House rule USR-004 is judged only by the reviewer: no check can decide it (The rule governs process and git history, not file content: it requires that when code already merged into master is edited additively, characterization tests are created BEFORE the edit. Deciding it needs (a) knowing whether the touched code is merged into master (including via ancestry), (b) judging whether a change is additive vs. intentional behaviour change, and (c) verifying commit/ordering that tests were authored first. A per-line regex cannot decide any of this, and no shell command can reliably distinguish a compliant additive change from a normal refactor without firing on legitimate work. Reviewers must judge this rule from commit history and PR ordering.).
House rule USR-005 is judged only by the reviewer: no check can decide it (Rule USR-005 is recorded only as a heading, "UI/UX, Form Intelligence, and Design System Standards". No requirement, forbidden element, required component/token, measurable limit or standard is stated, so there is nothing an automatic check could enforce: any regex or command would encode rules the owner never gave, and no reviewer has an order to judge against either. The rule needs its actual text (what the design system requires, what form intelligence means here, which standards apply) before checks can be written.).
